Author: Phex & Will Bickford (Nada Encoding); Verse (Base256 phonetic spec)
Maturity: spec
Last touched: 2026-02-28

A family of competing specs for pronouncing raw bytes as syllables, one syllable per byte: the Nada Encoding (3x5x17+1 = 256, with byte 0 as 'om', silence as a structural pause in computation), a 3x5x17+1 pronunciation guide with a Dolphin Variant using feminine vowels, and Verse's CVC system (16 initials x 4 vowels x 4 finals = 256) designed for unambiguous, error-detectable speech in noisy environments, with full 256-syllable lookup tables. Related to but distinct from the ALIVE Singular word maps and the phext-marked STT arc (this is byte-level spoken encoding, output side); no encoder/decoder tool shipped.

